# The vendored agent-ai bundle and its e2e copy are compared byte-for-byte and
# pinned by sha256 (scripts/agent-ai-vendor-copy.test.ts). Line-ending
# conversion on a Windows checkout would change those bytes and fail the pin
# with a message blaming the vendorer.
extensions/chrome/public/vendor/agent-ai.umd.js -text
e2e/fixtures/agent-ai.umd.js -text

# `-text` governs fresh checkouts. A clone made before this line keeps CRLF in
# its working tree and will still fail the sha pin. Do NOT `git add
# --renormalize`: with `-text` the clean filter converts nothing, so that
# stages the CRLF bytes as a new blob and changes the vendored bundle for
# everyone while the working tree — which the pin reads — stays wrong. Restore
# the bytes from the commit instead:
#
#   git checkout HEAD -- extensions/chrome/public/vendor/agent-ai.umd.js e2e/fixtures/agent-ai.umd.js
#
# `HEAD --` is the form that works in both states. A plain `git checkout --`
# restores from the index, which is fine while the index is clean — but once
# anything has staged those files (a stray `git add -A`, say) it holds the CRLF
# bytes and restores them again. Re-cloning works too.

# Shell scripts must reach the working tree with LF endings whatever a clone's
# `core.autocrlf` says. This repo's hooks run them, and bash does not tolerate
# the carriage returns: a fresh worktree on a machine with `autocrlf=true`
# checks out CRLF and every push then dies before a single test runs, with
#
#   scripts/fix-yalc.sh: line 2: : command not found
#   scripts/fix-yalc.sh: line 17: syntax error near unexpected token `in'
#
# The committed blobs are already LF; `eol=lf` is what stops the checkout
# rewriting them.
*.sh text eol=lf
.husky/* text eol=lf
